since his 1978 debut Michael Myers has  remained one of the original slasher  movie mascots after all he's already  survived multiple decades worth of  evolution retcons and reinventions  it's hard to believe that an evil force  of nature like Michael Myers could  actually exist based on the way Dr  Loomis talks about him throughout the  series Myers seems more like an ancient  figure from folklore than an actual  human being however director John  Carpenter credits the creation of  Michael Myers to a school trip where he  and his peers visited a mental Asylum  Carpenter discussed this fateful trip in  the documentary A Cut Above the Rest  saying we visited the most serious  mentally ill patients and there was this  kid he must have been 12 or 13 and he  literally had this look that image stuck  with Carpenter who Incorporated the idea  into the original Halloween film in  which a young Michael Myers stares into  the camera unmasked having committed a  heinous crime it's fairly common  knowledge that Michael Myers iconic mask  was actually an augmented William  Shatner mask in fact the script  contained no description of what Michael  Myers mask should actually look like  while on a panel at the Fright Night  Film Fest and horror weekend August 2008  filmmaker Tommy Lee Wallace who worked  behind the scenes on the original  Halloween explained the script asked for  a blank faced mask that makes you feel  eerie the killer was also never referred  to as Michael Myers and instead was  simply called the shape with no Specific  Instructions Wallace went to a costume  shop on Hollywood Boulevard looking for  the perfect mask he selected The Faded  William Shatner mask choosing the Emmett  Kelly clown character weary Willy is a  backup while they ultimately picked the  shatter mask young Michael does wear a  clown mask when murdering his sister  Wallace would later bring an eerie clown  to the screen when he directed the 1990  TV adaptation of Stephen King's It  since Michael Myers is just a guy in a  mask almost anyone could play the part  John Carpenter's longtime friend Nick  Castle is credited as the shape in the  first film but he wasn't the only actor  to play the role in 1978's Halloween  during the movie's opening a young actor  named will sandin plays a six-year-old  Michael who brutally murders his older  sister we see his face when the clown  mask is ripped away for a shocking  reveal during the climax of the film  Michael's mask is torn off again and  this time he's played by actor Tony  Moran however they weren't the only  actors responsible for bringing one of  Cinema's greatest Boogeyman to life in  the original movie in fact producer and  co-writer Deborah Hill had to step in to  play the role of six-year-old Michael's  hands the point of view sequence was so  complicated that filmmakers were unable  to bring in another child to be  Michael's heads in Adam rockoff's book  going to Pieces the rise and fall of the  slasher film 1978-19 6 Hill explained we  couldn't afford another kid to hold the  knife later that night so that's my hand  holding the knife that stabs Michael  Myers sister I had the littlest tense  the antagonist took a relationship  between Dr Loomis and Michael Myers  provides the Mythic undertones of the  Halloween series Loomis is determined to  end Michael's reign of evil especially  as it was Loomis who tried to treat  Michael following the original murder  basically no one knows Michael better  than Loomis if you don't find him in  four hours  I'm sure I will that connection runs  even deeper when you realize that the  actor who played Loomis Donald Pleasence  worked with stuntman dick warlock who  portrayed Michael in the original  Halloween 2 on The 1975 Disney film  Escape to Witch Mountain discussing his  time working with Pleasants warlock told  Halloween Daily News I found him to be a  gentleman people have said that he had a  sense of humor but I never saw it when  he came on set he was ready to work he  knew your dialogue and mine he was the  consummate professional you couldn't  find anybody who was better to work with  Halloween 3 season of the witch is  usually referred to as the Halloween  movie without Michael Myers on the  surface this is a totally fair statement  while Halloween and Halloween 2 are one  continuous story detailing the night  Michael Myers came home the third  installment was intended to take the  franchise in a totally different  direction in the documentary Standalone  the making of Halloween 3 season of The  Witch director Tommy Lee Wallace  explained the decision to create an  anthology movie saying each year let's  come out with a new movie on the subject  of Halloween All Hallows Eve and  everything that comes with it it could  go in any direction at once and each one  could spin off any number of their own  sequels and I don't know what the hell  is going on at the time the film wasn't  a hit with moviegoers and everyone's  favorite masked killer was brought back  in Halloween for the return of Michael  Myers still it could be said that Mars  presence is felt throughout the movie as  dick warlock who played Michael  Halloween 2 appears in a new role plus a  TV spot for the original 1978 film  featuring Michael Myers is shown on  television in Halloween 3. he may not be  the huge presence audiences were hoping  for but he still made it into the film  one of the few actors to play Michael  Myers more than once is George P Wilbur  who portrayed the shape in Halloween for  the return of Michael Myers and  Halloween the curse of Michael Myers  interestingly the stuntman credits John  Wayne with his career in Show Business  after serving in the Navy Wilbur got a  job at a Dude Ranch in Tucson telling  racks and razors they were filming the  movie El Dorado nearby so I went to the  set and got a job as an extra and a  stand-in for John Wayne shortly  afterward I moved to Los Angeles to  pursue a career as a stuntmen that  career certainly paid off as he went on  to add over 100 credits to his name and  got to play the shape on two separate  occasions Halloween 5 the Revenge of  Michael Myers is a flawed film to say  the least while the fourth installment  did an excellent job of bringing the  franchise back to basics part 5 took the  series into surreal territory one of the  movie's more bizarre Concepts depicts a  mysterious Men In Black with the same  tattoo as Michael roaming around  Haddonfield and breaking Michael out of  prison in the sixth film The Man in  Black was revealed to be Dr Terence Wynn  a character with only one scene in the  original Halloween I protect Michael  however that wasn't always the intention  in fact Don Shanks who played Michael in  the fifth film revealed that the movie's  director originally had different plans  for the character as Shanks told  Halloween Daily News I was going to be  the man in Black because I played him  too I did bits and pieces I didn't do  all of him but there would be scenes  where I'd go out one door here and I  just put on the coat and come through  the other door however this idea doesn't  make a lot of sense which is probably  why it was dropped as underwhelming as  the Dr Win reveal was having Michael be  the man in Black would have just been  plain confusing  in 1998 Halloween H2O 20 years later  came out rebooting the franchise and  creating a brand new timeline in the  process the idea to continue Laurie  strode's story two decades on actually  came from Jamie Lee Curtis herself as  she told Entertainment Weekly in 2018  when Halloween was like 19 years old I  remember calling John Carpenter and  Deborah Hill and we had lunch I said to  them guys the movie is going to be 20  years old next year and we're all still  doing the job 20 years later I said to  them why don't we revisit it Halloween  H2O ignored everything that took place  after Halloween 2 and it was supposed to  depict Lori taking down Michael Myers  once and for all as the project  continued however Curtis started reading  scripts that left Michael's demise more  ambiguous that's when she learned that  producer Mustafa alakad who had control  over the character included a clause in  the contract that said Michael Myers  cannot be killed a compromise made and a  new ending was produced in which Lori  appears to chop Michael's head off the  idea was it would make the audience feel  like Michael is finally officially dead  but it could also easily be explained  away in a sequel indeed its successor  Halloween resurrection revealed that  Laurie had actually killed a man that  Michael had dressed in his clothes  allowing the murderer to escape sadly  the audience of Halloween H2O was robbed  of a truly satisfying ending oh my God  she killed the wrong person when the  time came to officially reboot the  Halloween franchise in 2007 Rob Zombie  was brought in to remake the original  film in his own Vision he decided his  Michael Myers should be the most  visually intimidating Michael to date so  he cast the six foot nine inch actor and  former professional wrestler Tyler Maine  although his face is obscured throughout  most of the film some viewers recognized  him as Sabretooth from 2000's X-Men  zombies 2007 remake of Halloween and its  2009 follow-up are much darker and more  brutal than the original aside from a  handful of scary moments a young person  could watch John Carpenter's Halloween  and walkway relatively unscathed zombies  reboot however is incredibly violent and  crude so much so that even the child  actor playing a young Michael Myers in  the movie wasn't allowed to read the  entire script during an interview with  MovieWeb to promote the film actor Jake  Ferrick explained I only read my part  and then my mom read it and then she  said you're not gonna read that and once  the film was finished ferric still  wasn't allowed to see it the young actor  revealed that he would be allowed to  watch the beginning of the film but  would have to leave the premiere and go  do something else after that on account  of zombies flick being too gruesome I am  going to be kept away even for the  premiere I'm gonna watch maybe the first  10 15 minutes given the film's onslaught  of profanity grotesque violence and  grisly murders it's easy to see why  fairc wasn't allowed to watch the entire  movie despite playing such a major role  2018's Halloween which was directed by  David Gordon green gloriously rebooted  the franchise by largely ignoring all of  its sequels even Halloween 2 the  filmmakers worked hard to honor the  original 1978 film by bringing back  Jamie Lee Curtis and enlisting John  Carpenter to score the new movie in  another important nod for hardcore fans  Nick Castle was also brought back to  occasionally embody the role of Michael  Myers which he helped originate although  Castle didn't play the character for any  large stretch of time in 2018's  Halloween fans loved his inclusion in  the movie although Castle wasn't on set  for the filming of Halloween kills his  voice or should we say breath was used  extensively as James Jude Courtney the  actor and stuntman who portrays Michael  Myers in Green's Trilogy told Halloween  Daily News so when I'm stalking you and  you hear the breathing in The Mask it's  Nick Castle breathing which I think  again is very poetic because Nick is the  spirit of this character it's Nick's  spirit that I reached in and grabbed  manifest the 2018 character  [Music]
 

